  "articleBody": "Deciding whether to repair or completely replace a handrail can be tricky, but there are clear signs that point to replacement being the safer, more cost-effective option in the long run. If your handrail's looseness suggests a structural failure, like significant damage to the rail itself, the newel post, or the wall it's anchored to, a repair might only be a temporary band-aid. We often see this when the anchor points in masonry or damaged framing are beyond simple repair. Another key factor is code compliance. If your current handrail doesn't meet height or clearance requirements, or if altering it could trigger local building control notifications, a full replacement allows you to bring it up to current safety standards. This is especially important for properties in areas like Denver, Aurora, and Colorado Springs that are undergoing inspections for sale or insurance purposes. Finally, consider accessibility modifications; if the existing rail can't be adapted to meet new needs, replacement is usually the best route. When in doubt, it’s always wise to get a professional opinion to ensure the long-term safety and compliance of your stairs.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}